  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n500H ridge over the Tennessee Valley region will continue to provide\nmass subsidence and hot conditions across the area. Fresh guidance\npaints heat indices around 100F for most areas along and south of the\nI-70 corridor on Friday afternoon. Additionally, guidance came in a\ntad warmer for areas along the OH/IN border, so have expanded the\nheat headline accordingly. The ridge will continue to be squashed by\na trough moving through eastern Canada, resulting in slightly cooler\ntemperatures across central and eastern Ohio. \n\nThere is some question as to whether an extension to the Heat\nAdvisory will need to be made for portions of the Tri-State for \nSaturday. However, decided to hold off for now given uncertainty with\nstorms and cloud blowoff (see more below). \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nAs we head through the daytime hours on Friday, the disturbance in\nCanada begins to deepen and stronger northwesterly flow begins to \nencroach into the area. This shift puts us moreso into MCS territory \nin terms of shear and driving flow. Of course, timing out MCS arrival\nis notoriously difficult and often depends on previous MCS \ndevelopment/decay and residual outflow boundaries. \n\nHandful of CAMs pull a complex through central Ohio through Friday\nafternoon while others keep us entirely dry. In addition to that\nambiguity, there also isn't a strong consensus between run to run of\nvarious CAMs either. As such, once again, we have a low confidence \nforecast. However, if we do end up with afternoon thunderstorm \ndevelopment, SBCAPE on the order of 2500 J/kg, steep LL lapse rates, \nsplotchy bulk shear around 30-35 knots, and PWATs around 180% of \nnormal introduce a severe wind and locally heavy rainfall threat. \nGreatest confidence in this occurring is once again through central \nOhio\n\nDepending on the activity Friday, another round or two of storms \nwould be possible Friday night into Saturday morning with additional \nshortwave impulses rounding the base of the larger flow. As noted in \nprevious discussions, multiple thunderstorm complexes are possible \nacross the local area up until Saturday evening. Drier air will \neventually work into the region from the north as the ridge gets \nsuppressed to the west. \n\nWith PWATs well above normal, multiple thunderstorm complexes will\nprovide the opportunity for locally heavy rainfall and flash\nflooding, especially for areas where storms move over repeatedly.",
